<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Classroom News – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Grade:</b> 6th Grade (CEFR: A2.1)</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Classroom News</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Theme:</b> We Need Pictures for the Classroom Newsletter.</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</p></td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</p></td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Write specific comments to peers to improve a writing product in the form of feedback.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can compose descriptive texts about recent class events (e.g., 'Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the museum.').
            Can write constructive feedback for classmates' newsletter articles, including compliments and suggestions (e.g., 'Your story about the school trip is fun to read. You can add more details about the activities.').
            Can write suggestions for content (e.g., 'We need more pictures for the classroom newsletter.').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows a projected image of a blank classroom newsletter template. Teacher asks: "What do we need for our classroom newsletter?" Students brainstorm ideas. Teacher writes key vocabulary on the board (e.g., 'article', 'pictures', 'headline', 'editor', 'topic'). Teacher asks: "What did we write about yesterday?" (Connecting to past lesson's topic for the newsletter).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher presents a short, simple sample article (e.g., "Yesterday, we went on a field trip to the art museum. It was fun. We saw many paintings.") and models giving constructive feedback using a simple sentence frame: "Your story about the field trip is fun to read. You can add more details about the activities." The teacher writes this feedback on the board. Teacher also models emphasizing the main verb in future tense contractions when saying possible next steps, e.g., "I'll *write* more details." or "They'll *take* more pictures." The teacher points out short /ɑ/ in 'art' and long /oʊ/ in 'show' from previous lesson vocabulary.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is this feedback helpful?" (Students respond: Yes/No)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Does this feedback just say 'good' or 'bad'?" (Students respond: No, it says what is good and what can be better.)</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Short narrative excerpt and feedback examples. The teacher projects a short, previously read article about a class event (e.g., "Yesterday, we had a presentation about healthy food. It was exciting. Students showed colorful posters."). Below it, the teacher shows two examples of written feedback: one effective and one ineffective, focusing on compliments and suggestions. The teacher asks: "Which feedback is better? Why?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Describe plans for events; Request materials. Sociolinguistic: Collaborate in writing projects, clarifying roles and responsibilities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to the better feedback and asks: "What does this feedback tell us about the article?" (Gist question: It says it's good and how to make it better.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher shows statements about the feedback and students identify them as True/False, e.g., "The good feedback only says 'This is good.'" (False) or "The good feedback suggests adding more details." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher highlights phrases like 'Your story is creative...' and 'You can add...' explaining that this is how we give positive comments and suggestions. "We need pictures for the classroom newsletter" will be presented as a common suggestion.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Writing)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing constructive feedback using present simple for compliments, 'can add' for suggestions, and present/past simple for describing content. Using vocabulary: 'article', 'pictures', 'event', 'add', 'describe', 'exciting', 'creative'. Grammar focus: Present simple with questions ('What else do we need?'), Future in affirmative ('I will write...') and interrogative ('What will you do?'), Past simple ('Yesterday, we went...').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                <ol>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Controlled Practice - Sentence Completion:</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with partially completed feedback sentences based on short sample 'articles'. Students complete the sentences using given vocabulary and target grammar.
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 1: "Your _______ about the ______ is _______. You _______ more details about ______." (Students fill: 'article', 'event', 'exciting', 'can add', 'what happened').</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Example 2: "I like ______ headline. ______ need more _______ for the ______." (Students fill: 'your', 'We', 'pictures', 'newsletter').</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Semi-controlled Practice - Guided Feedback Writing:</b> Students receive a new, very short, simple article (e.g., "Last week, our class had an art activity. We made colorful posters. It was fun.") and a feedback template. The template has two sections: "I like..." and "Suggestion: You can... / We need...". Students write one compliment and one suggestion for the article, focusing on using the target vocabulary and grammar structures. Teacher encourages questions like "What did this article describe?" (Past simple) or "What will the writer do next?" (Future simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaborative Writing - Peer Feedback Exchange:</b> In pairs, students exchange their short articles from the previous activity. Each student reads their partner's article and then writes constructive feedback directly on their partner's paper or on a separate feedback sheet. They must include at least one compliment using an adjective (e.g., 'creative', 'fun') and at least one specific suggestion for improvement (e.g., 'add more details', 'take a photo', 'describe another event'). Students use phrases like "Your topic is great!" and "You can add a photo here." or "We need more pictures for the classroom newsletter." This uses the sociolinguistic competence to collaborate.</li>
                </ol>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Written feedback on a peer's draft newsletter article.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students receive a classmate's draft article (from a previous lesson or a pre-prepared set of drafts from the teacher about recent class events, such as 'Yesterday, we had a special presentation.') for the "Our Classroom News" newsletter.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each student will act as an 'editor' and write a detailed constructive feedback note to their peer. The feedback note must include:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">One compliment about the article (e.g., "Your article about the event is very clear!").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">At least two specific suggestions for improvement, focusing on adding information or suggesting visual elements (e.g., "You can add more details about the teacher's role." or "We need more pictures for the classroom newsletter to make it colorful.").</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">A question using present simple or future tense related to the content or next steps (e.g., "What else do we need to know about the topic?" or "What will you do to revise it?").</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students should use target vocabulary ('article', 'newsletter', 'pictures', 'event', 'add', 'describe', 'edit', 'creative', 'exciting') and grammatical structures (present simple questions, future tense, past simple for describing the article's content).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher circulates, offering support and checking for appropriate language use (e.g., "I will write the article. Can you check it?").</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative assessment)</p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist and collection of written feedback notes for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit the written feedback notes they prepared for their peers.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Specificity:</b> Feedback clearly communicates what is good and what needs improvement.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Includes at least 3-4 target words (e.g., 'article', 'pictures', 'add', 'describe', 'creative').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Accuracy:</b> Correct use of present simple, past simple, and future simple sentences in feedback.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Completeness:</b> Includes at least one compliment and two specific suggestions.</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students write their answers to the following prompts on a small slip of paper:
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One thing I learned about writing constructive feedback today is..."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can help my classmates improve their articles by..."</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p></td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How was giving feedback different from just saying 'good' or 'bad'?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of writing feedback was most challenging for you today?"</li>
                </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> What activities worked best for practicing written feedback? Were students able to use the target grammar (present simple for questions, future tense, past simple) effectively within their feedback? Did they use the recommended vocabulary? What adjustments are needed for students struggling with writing specific suggestions? Were the activities engaging and collaborative? (e.g., "I will be responsible for the layout of the page." 'Can you write the article about our school trip?' 'Let's decide who will collect the pictures.')</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> The written feedback from today's lesson will be used in Lesson 5 (Mediation & Integration). Students will revise their own articles based on the feedback received and consolidate information for the final classroom newsletter project, possibly acting as "editors" to summarize feedback or prepare for the layout.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2-3 min</p></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>